BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gabrielle Drolet: Look Ma\, No Hands
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T200000
UID:69f4c76e1d82f930d3cb4ca1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Gabrielle Drolet\nIn 2021\, Gabrielle Drolet developed a condi
 tion that made her unable to use her hands. It only worsened over time\, a
 nd as a writer and artist\, she had to learn new ways of creating and expr
 essing herself. The experience completely changed her life and her outlook
 .\nLook Ma\, No Hands\n(2025) explores both the difficulty and the humour 
 of developing chronic and life-altering pain in her twenties. Each chapter
  looks at a different aspect of her life touched by her disability: how sh
 e learned to write when she couldn’t type\; how she learned to manage th
 e most mundane daily tasks. She moves cities and as her work as a writer a
 nd cartoonist builds has to navigate different byzantine health systems wi
 thout the privilege or security of having a family doctor\, even as she mo
 ves into her new apartment and embarks on first dates. And she does all of
  this with the most wonderful sense of the absurd.\nLook Ma\, No Hands\nis
  utterly charming and shares profound reflections on life’s curveballs\,
  and explores how\, in Drolet’s words\, “you can live a full—even fu
 nny—life in a disabled body.”\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nGabrielle D
 rolet\n(she/her) is a bisexual journalist\, essayist\, and cartoonist base
 d in Montreal. She is a regular contributor to\nThe New Yorker\,\nshe cont
 ributes cartoons to the\nGlobe and Mail\n\, and she has been a guest panel
 ist on CBC’s\nCommotion\n. Her written work has been published in the\nN
 ew York Times\,\nthe\nGlobe and Mail\, The Walrus\, VICE\, Teen Vogue\n\, 
 and more. Her essays on disability have been nominated for a Digital Publi
 shing Award and won gold at the Canadian Online Publishing Awards. Gabriel
 le holds an MFA from the University of Guelph.\nAnu Radha Verma\n(sh\ne/he
 r) is a cis\, queer\, woman of colour from Mississauga. She co-founded QTB
 IPOC sauga\, a grassroots gathering for QTBIPOC folks in and from the Peel
  Region. She has written and spoken on the histories and present realities
  of queer community organizing in the suburbs. Anu Radha is a curator\, wi
 th a history in performance events such as brOWN//out and QueeriAHcity. Sh
 e has worked in community-based and non-profit organizations in both Canad
 a and India\, focused on various dimensions of social justice including he
 alth equity\, climate change\, gender-based violence\, racism and xenophob
 ia. She recently was a fellow in the Dalla Lana Fellowship in Journalism a
 nd Health Impact\, and works as a freelance journalist. Anu Radha is mad-i
 dentified\, lives with chronic fatigue\, and is a survivor.\n**\nPLAN YOUR
  VISIT:\nLocation:\nBeeton Hall\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Str
 eet)\, 1st floor\nTime:\nBook signing starts at 6:00pm. Program starts at 
 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversel
 l these events to make sure that the greatest number of people have an opp
 ortunity to attend. Tickets are guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity 
 is reached. Any remaining space will be opened up to the rush line beginni
 ng at 6:45pm. Seating first come\, first served. Tickets do not guarantee 
 seats.\n**\nThis event is a part of spotlight Pride programming. Visit\ntp
 l.ca/pride\nfor more listings.\n**\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pe
 rsonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library
  under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The 
 information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliver
 y and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information
  can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-598
 1\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.\n**\nAccessibi\nlity at this pr
 ogram:\nThis program will be presented with ASL interpretation. Additional
  supports are outlined below.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f4c76e1d82f930d3cb4ca1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
