BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Terry Tempest Williams: The Glorians
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260617T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260617T200000
UID:69a0bdc2491b809c6f1cc07d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this time of political fragility\, climate chaos\, and seek
 ing beauty wherever we can find its glimmer\, Terry Tempest Williams intro
 duces us to the Glorians. They are not distant deities\, but the ordinary\
 , often overlooked presences – animal\, plant\, memory\, moment – that
  reveal our shared vulnerability and interconnectedness with the natural w
 orld. The Glorians can be as small as an ant ferrying a coyote willow blos
 som to its queen or as commonplace as the night sky. But what they can col
 lectively show us – about the radical act of attending to beauty and car
 rying forward against all odds – is immense.\nJourneying through encount
 ers with the Glorians in the red rock desert of Utah during the pandemic t
 o Harvard University where she teaches in the Divinity School\, Williams w
 eaves a story of astonishing personal and societal insight. Join her for t
 his rare discussion as she grapples with the unsettled state of the world\
 , and turns not to despair but to deep reflection.\nPlease note there will
  not be a book signing at this event\; however\, there will be signed copi
 es of\nThe Glorians\navailable for purchase.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\n
 Terry Tempest Williams\nis the award-winning author of over twenty books o
 f creative nonfiction\, including the environmental classic\,\nRefuge: An 
 Unnatural History of Family and Place\n. Among her other books are\nLeap\n
 \;\nRed\n\;\nThe Open Space of Democracy\n\;\nFinding Beauty in a Broken W
 orld\n\;\nWhen Women Were Birds\n\;\nThe Hour of Land\n\; and\nErosion: Es
 says of Undoing\n. Her work has been translated and anthologized worldwide
 . A recipient of a Guggenheim Fellowship and a Lannan Literary Award\, she
  is a member of the American Academy of Arts & Letters and is currently wr
 iter-in-residence at the Harvard Divinity School. She divides her time bet
 ween Cambridge\, Massachusetts and Southeastern Utah.\n***\nThis event is 
 part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internatio
 nal authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books a
 nd big ideas.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salo
 n\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors
  open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.
 \nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to make sure that the greatest 
 number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets are guaranteed unt
 il 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Any remaining space will be opened
  up to the rush line beginning at 6:45pm. Seating first come\, first serve
 d. Tickets do not guarantee seats.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to ac
 cessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to reques
 t accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca 
 or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation
  Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by
  the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a
 )\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pro
 gram registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted
  to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
  answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, 2nd Floo
 r
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0bdc2491b809c6f1cc07d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Toronto Baseball Tales: Birth of the Blue Jays & The Curse of the 
 Seagull
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T130000
UID:69a0c4be8b29ea2800d2b893@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:More than a century after the city's first baseball game\, the
  Major Leagues finally came to Toronto in 1977.\nIn this talk\, storytelle
 r and historian Adam Bunch concludes this lecture series by following the 
 Blue Jays' rise from a struggling expansion team to World Series Champions
 \, a story that features kidnapping\, heartbreak\, a bizarre curse\, and a
  prophetic dream that changed baseball history.\nAbout this event's guest:
 \nAdam Bunch\nRead More:\nThe Toronto Book of Love\nThe Toronto Book of th
 e Dead\nAbout this series:\nBaseball was being played in Toronto more than
  a century before the Blue Jays were born. In this series of talks\, we'll
  explore the game's evolution in our city\, from the days when it was a wo
 rking class sport played by "undesirables" to Joe Carter jumping for joy i
 n front of 50\,000 screaming fans. Along the way\, we'll meet everyone fro
 m con artists and kidnappers to eccentric millionaires and feminist icons\
 , the people who've made Toronto baseball what it is… and helped transfo
 rm our city in the process.\n***\nImportant information for accessing this
  live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite t
 o gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webin
 ar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and 
 Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar 
 link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed i
 n to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to book 
 your ticket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event links 
 can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto P
 ublic Library\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to as man
 y Torontonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or hav
 e a disability and would like to request accommodation to participate in t
 his program. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we will 
 do our best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is preferre
 d. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInformation
  Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by
  the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a
 )\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pro
 gram registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted
  to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
  answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0c4be8b29ea2800d2b893
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260620T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260620T130000
UID:69a0e9296724c63d00f579a6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:WRAP is for\nanyone\nlooking to strengthen their well-being—
 whether you’re supporting your mental health\, managing stress\, or simp
 ly wanting to feel more balanced and resilient in daily life. Participant
 s will receive a\nWRAP certificate\nupon program completion.\nIn an 8-wee
 k WRAP group\, individuals explore:\nself-care tools like peer connections
 \, relaxation and mindfulness techniques\, stress reduction strategies\, a
 nd more\nresources for keeping well and feeling better in difficult times.
 \nInterested in learning more? Drop in for the information session on Sat
 urday\, June 13\, 2026\, 10 am-12 pm.\nThis WRAP Group meets\nevery Saturd
 ay\,\nonce a week for 8 weeks\, June 20-August 15\, 2026\, 10 am-1 pm\n. 
 There will be no session on Saturday\, August 1. Registration closes afte
 r week 3\, or when full.\nTTC Presto tickets will be provided.\nSnacks and
  refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will be
  available at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGerste
 in Crisis Centre's WRAP initiative\n. Gerstein Crisis Centre is solely res
 ponsible for the protection of \npersonal information collected through th
 e program registration \nprocess.
LOCATION:Maria A. Shchuka\, Children's Program Room - Main floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a0e9296724c63d00f579a6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
