BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sameer Farooq and Dan Hicks: Restitution and Reimagining Collectio
 ns
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T200000
UID:69c148e4567ba1dd31fe3d6a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL in partnership with the Toronto Biennial of Art and Canada
 <->International\, present artist Sameer Farooq and curator and author Dan
  Hicks in conversation with Tandazani Dhlakama at the North York Central L
 ibrary's Concourse Event Space.\nMuseums have long been sites of contested
  memory\, institutions that claim to preserve culture while often displayi
 ng the spoils of colonialism. Collections assembled through empire sit beh
 ind glass\, their violent origins obscured by curatorial labels. Objects t
 orn from their communities are renamed "artifacts" and their stories rewri
 tten. But a growing movement is asking: who has the right to collect\, to 
 display\, to tell these stories? And what would it mean to fundamentally r
 eimagine how museums relate to the communities they claim to serve?\nArtis
 t Sameer Farooq’s\,\nFlatbread Library\n\, a large-scale sculpture mappi
 ng Toronto's diversity through flatbreads from bakeries across the city\, 
 was a standout of the 2024 Toronto Biennial of Art. His work explores how 
 everyday objects carry stories of migration\, belonging\, and cultural ide
 ntity. Oxford University professor Dan Hicks\, author of the groundbreakin
 g\nThe Brutish Museums\nand the newly released\nEvery Monument Will Fall: 
 A Story of Remembering and Forgetting\n(named one of Art Forum's Books of 
 2025)\, has become one of the world's leading voices on cultural restituti
 on\, challenging museums to reckon with their colonial foundations. ROM Cu
 rator of Global Africa Tandazani Dhlakama actively upends traditional coll
 ecting practices as she takes stock of and reimagines the future of the RO
 M's Global Africa collection.\nThis conversation emerges from Farooq and H
 icks's recent time together during Farooq’s residency at Worlding\, UK (
 in collaboration with Canada <-> International)\, where they spent time at
  the Pitt Rivers Museum examining collections and discussing shared concer
 ns about restitution\, cultural ownership\, and the ethics of display. How
  does the movement and migration of objects\, both historical and contempo
 rary\, inform how we create museum displays? What does it mean to decoloni
 ze not just labels but the fundamental act of collecting itself? How do we
  move beyond simply acknowledging colonial violence to actually returning 
 what was taken and engaging in meaningful reconciliation?\nAs monuments fa
 ll and museums face mounting pressure to reckon with their pasts\, these t
 hree visionaries offer crucial perspectives on what comes next: new ways o
 f contributing to\, building\, and exhibiting collections that honor rathe
 r than extract\, that build relationships rather than reinforce hierarchie
 s\, that imagine museums as sites of repair rather than repositories of th
 eft.\nThe 4th edition of the Toronto Biennial of Art opens on September 26
 \, 2026.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nSameer Farooq\nDan Hicks\nTandazan
 i Dhlakama\nRead more:\nEvery Monument Will Fall\nThe Brutish Museums\n***
 \nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event Space\, North York Central 
 Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level.\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event s
 tarts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe o
 versell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people have an 
 opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity i
 s reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is comm
 itted to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would lik
 e to request accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservi
 ces@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\n
 This event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local 
 and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about the
 ir new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pers
 onal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library u
 nder the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The in
 formation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery 
 and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey fo
 llowing the program. Questions about how the library handles your informat
 ion can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-598
 1\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69c148e4567ba1dd31fe3d6a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Darragh McGee: Imitation Games
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T200000
UID:69c15d2e07a74a47597a6ba1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents sociologist and BBC New Generation Thinker Darrag
 h McGee in conversation with at the Toronto Reference Library's Atrium.\nI
 n his eye-opening new book\nImitation Games: How the Gambling Industry Hij
 acked Sport\n\, University of Bath sociologist Darragh McGee travels the w
 orld to tell the incredible story of how online gambling transformed from 
 a provincial cottage industry twenty-five years ago into a billion dollar 
 business. He traces the individuals and companies who brought this brave n
 ew world of betting into being\, and got very rich doing so. But he also d
 ocuments the devastation left behind: the inadequate safeguards\, the brok
 en lives\, the moment when the fun stops.\nWhat does this mean for the fut
 ure of sport and society? As Canada embraces legal online gambling\, with 
 Ontario leading the charge\, we're seeing the same patterns that devastate
 d problem gamblers elsewhere\, now playing out here. The industry profits 
 only when people lose\, yet it has successfully rebranded gambling as harm
 less entertainment\, fan engagement\, a way to feel closer to the action. 
 Meanwhile\, vulnerable populations\, young men especially\, face unprecede
 nted risk of addiction.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available f
 or purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nDarragh McGee\nRead more:\nImi
 tation Games : How Gambling Hijacked Sports\n***\nThis event is part of ou
 r signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international author
 s\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big ide
 as.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nAtrium\, Toronto Reference Library 
 (789 Yonge Street)\, 1st floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:
 00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell 
 these events to make sure that the greatest number of people have an oppor
 tunity to attend. Tickets are guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is
  reached. Any remaining space will be opened up to the rush line beginning
  at 6:45pm. Seating first come\, first served. Tickets do not guarantee se
 ats.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf o
 r have a disability and would like to request accommodation for this event
 \, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three
  weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe perso
