SUMMARY:Finding a Job in Canada with ACCES Employment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260624T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260624T150000
UID:69f39df2fb87a3c2e2ace234@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us in this interactive workshop to learn how to plan and 
 adapt your job search to the Canadian market\, utilize tools for job searc
 h and networking in Canada\, create a modern resume\, and gain insights in
 to today’s Canadian workplace culture.\nRegistration is encouraged on TP
 L website.\nDrop-ins welcome until capacity is reached.\nInformation Colle
 ction Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the T
 oronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20
 (c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program r
 egistration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library han
 dles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@t
 pl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f39df2fb87a3c2e2ace234
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Fadi Shasha
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260501T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260531T235959
UID:69f3a04a762d19c7d510e74e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of May\, the works of artist Fadi Shasha
  are on display at Albion Branch.\nFadi Shasha is a doctor who found tim
 e to express love for nature and art.\n"I believe in exploring and expandi
 ng humans ability in expressing self through love of art and encouraging o
 thers to follow suit. My current art work is digital painting with printe
 d pictures that were painted with iPad and inspired from other painting or
  photos but not copied."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Space
 \nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exhi
 bit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect
  the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe e
 xhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check the\n
 Albion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credi
 t: Artist: Fadi Shasha\; Bouquet of Flowers
LOCATION:Albion\, Albion Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3a04a762d19c7d510e74e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zaiba Baig: Kainchee Lagaa & Jhooti
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260616T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260616T200000
UID:69f3a6cbcdd2f7e31fe3c274@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Zaiba Baig (photo credit: Maxholio)\nJoin playwright and autho
 r Zaiba Baig for a conversation about\nKainchee Lagaa & Jhooti: The Beggin
 g Brown Bitch Plays\n\, two daring and subversive dramas that challenge th
 e white savior complex and blur the line between truth and performance thr
 ough unreliable narrators.\nIn Kainchee Lagaa\, two estranged siblings rec
 onnect across space and time as painful memories and family trauma resurfa
 ce. In\nJhooti\n\, a trans woman on the run\, carrying a plastic bag full
  of her belongings\, pleads for protection. But soon her story unravels in
 to a dangerous web of destruction. Both plays plunge into the multidimensi
 onal depths of Brown trans women teetering on a tightrope between truth an
 d survival.\nBaig will discuss the themes\, characters and creative proces
 s behind these explosive works\, and how the plays use theatrical disrupti
 on to confront audiences and challenge dominant narratives.\nABOUT THIS EV
 ENT'S GUESTS:\nZaiba Baig\n(she/they) is a writer\, actor\, and producer f
 or stage and screen\, as well as a workshop developer and facilitator for
  art-focused non-profits. Baig’s published works include their first pla
 y\,\nAcha Bacha\n(2020\, nominated for the Dayne Ogilvie prize from the Wr
 iter’s Trust of Canada)\, and an anthology of monologues they co-edited 
 for queer/trans artists titled\nThis Is Beyond\n(2023). Baig was a co-crea
 tor\, executive producer\, and lead actor in their Peabody Award–winning
  series\nSort Of\n(2021–2023) for which Baig received the Canadian Scree
 n Award for comedy writing and performance\, as well as a Gotham Award nom
 ination for performance in a new series. Baig has facilitated creative wri
 ting and playwriting programs for trans artists\, Black\, Indigenous\, and
  People of Colour (BIPOC) artists\, and trans youth including\, Trans Gem
 mes (2019 2020)\, Paprika Festival’s Playwrights Unit (2019–2023)\, Be
 ing Me (2023–)\, and Dialogue Dolls (2023).\nNedra Rodrigo\n(she/they) 
 is the founder of the Tamil Studies Symposium at York University and the q
 ueer-positive bilingual BlPOC event series\, The Tam Fam Lit Jam. She is 
 a translator\, academic\, curator of multi-arts events and advisor for the
  Tamil Community Centre Project in Scarborough. Nedra’s published transl
 ations include\nIn the Shadow of the Sword\nand\nthe\nDevakanthan quintet\
 nPrison of Dreams\nfor which she received the Tamil Literary Garden’s Tr
 anslation Award for 2024. Her essays and translations have appeared in sev
 eral prominent journals and anthologies\n.\nShe co-edited the anthology\nT
 amil Terrains\nwith Geetha Sukumaran. She is a recipient of the PEN Transl
 ates SALT Award for 2025 for her translations of Rashmy’s collection of 
 poems\nSongs in a Time of Confinement\n. She has conducted workshops in co
 mmunity capacity building\, literature\, archives\, and translation in sch
 ools\, universities and community.\n**\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation\n: Beet
 on Hall\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 1st floor\nTime:\
 nBook signing starts at 6:00pm. Program starts at 7:00pm.\nTickets\n: Free
  registration required.\nTicket policy\n: We oversell these events to make
  sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Ti
 ckets are guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Any remain
 ing space will be opened up to the rush line beginning at 6:45pm. Seating 
 first come\, first served. Tickets do not guarantee seats.\n**\nThis event
  is a part of spotlight Pride programming. Visit\ntpl.ca/pride\nfor more l
 istings.\n**\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on 
 this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libr
 aries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only 
 be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nReg
 istrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program
 \n. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direct
 ed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Str
 eet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall (1st Floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3a6cbcdd2f7e31fe3c274
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:From Balconies to Backyards: Planning a Successful Vegetable Garde
 n
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T193000
UID:69f3a620129f1274383bd812@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Want to enjoy vegetables you've grown yourself in your own bac
 kyard or on your balcony? A Toronto Master Gardener will lead a demonstrat
 ion and show how to get started to grow your own vegetables. Get some tips
  and learn techniques for successful growing. At the end of the program\, 
 vegetable seeds or seedlings will be shared with attendees\, while supplie
 s last.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3a620129f1274383bd812
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Astigmatic Variations by Ross Stockwell
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260601T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T235959
UID:69f3c3a91d82f930d3cb2ce1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of June\, the works of artist Ross Stockw
 ell are on display at\nDeer Park branch\n.\nRoss Stockwell is based in Tor
 onto\, Ontario. Borne of a mindful presence\, he notices whimsy and intrig
 ue in unlikely places. His work is eclectic\, unified by illuminating odd
 ities and the play of geometric forms. He expresses subjective experience\
 , exploring states of being\, felt-sense moods\, and emotions in an inani
 mate context. Stockwell has shown his work at Spectra (May 2022\, Hutchiso
 n Road)\; G44’s Members Gallery (January 2023\; Dissolution) and Spectr
 a\, May 2024\; Astigmatic Variations)\n"Astigmatic Variations” is an ong
 oing project where Stockwell captures images using time exposures and in-c
 amera movement. Textured overlays are applied to render artistic interpre
 tations depicting mood states of longing\, melancholy\, and dissolution\, 
 often implicit in our experience of time and imperfection. Stockwell has 
 an appreciation for the immediate perception of things while being aware o
 f their implied history and their dynamic trajectory toward some future."
 \nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Space\nprogram. Toronto Publi
 c Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at locati
 ons across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural in
 terests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed dur
 ing Deer Park's open hours. Please check the\nDeer Park branch\npage befo
 re your visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nSolitary Figure\nImage credit: Art
 ist: Ross Stockwell\nTitle of Piece: Solitary Figure\nMedium: Photo-Inkje
 t Print - Framed\nSize/Dimensions: Medium
LOCATION:Deer Park
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3c3a91d82f930d3cb2ce1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Club: I Hope We Choose Love
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T160000
UID:69f633408ddc1caf5f20a2ef@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In a heartbreaking yet hopeful collection of personal essays a
 nd prose poems\, blending the confessional\, political\, and literary\, Ka
 i Cheng Thom dives deep into the questions that haunt social movements tod
 ay. With the author's characteristic eloquence and honesty\,\nI Hope We Ch
 oose Love\nproposes heartfelt solutions on the topics of violence\, compli
 city\, family\, vengeance\, and forgiveness.\nJoin us for a chat about the
  book I Hope We Choose Love by Kai Cheng Thom\nThis is a registered progra
 m\, you can register online and pick up a copy of the book in branch.
LOCATION:St. Clair/Silverthorn
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f633408ddc1caf5f20a2ef
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google Prompting Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T160000
UID:69fe50911773192800186f3d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Ready to develop the skills to use generative AI (GenAI) tools
  effectively and responsibly for work and daily life? Join our staff-facil
 itated learning circle to complete the self-paced Google Prompting Essenti
 als course. By completing this program\, you will earn a Google Prompting 
 Essentials certificate to share on your resume\, LinkedIn and social media
  profiles!\nWhat You'll Learn\nThis program is designed for those with som
 e experience with using GenAI. Through short videos\, articles\, assignmen
 ts and quizzes\, you will discover:\nEssential\nPrompting Skills\n: How to
  use a prompting framework to create a desired output with GenAI\; how to 
 evaluate and refine your results.\nEveryday Work Tasks:\nStrategies to dra
 ft content\, brainstorm ideas\, create tables\, timelines\, and more.\nDat
 a & Presentations:\nHow to explore data visualization and use GenAI respon
 sibly when working with data.\nAdvanced Techniques:\nHow to use prompt cha
 ining for complex tasks and prompt versioning to save and reuse your best 
 prompts over time.\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary s
 taff for people who want to complete the Google Prompting Essentials speci
 alization together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work to
 gether through course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to a
 ttending all four learning circle sessions.\nSome Experience with GenAI Re
 commended: This course is ideal for participants who have completed the Go
 ogle AI Essentials course Learning Circle.\nHow to Joi\nn a Learning Circl
 e:\nRegister once on the first session of this event series\, in order to 
 attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid email address\n.\nA val
 id TPL library card is required for online circles.\nAttend all four ses
 sions (online via Zoom\, Fridays from June 5-June 26 at 1:30 pm)\nRegistra
 tion is a two-step procedure. First\, participants must complete their reg
 istration through this page. After this step\, library staff will follow u
 p to request additional information in order to complete the registration.
 \nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is
  collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, 
 sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for 
 event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about 
 how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline sta
 ff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON 
 M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69fe50911773192800186f3d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
