BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cliffcrest Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260624T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260624T150000
UID:6a0e0a0c8ea300e2631749cc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Cliffcrest Book Club meets on the 4th Wednesday of every 
 month. Dive into a great read\, share your thoughts\, and enjoy lively dis
 cussions. Whether you're a bookworm or just looking for a fun way to explo
 re new genres\, all are welcome!\nThis month we'll be reading:\nThe Dutch 
 House by Anne Patchett\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Cliffcrest
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a0e0a0c8ea300e2631749cc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Classic Twists: Never Let Me Go
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260612T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260612T130000
UID:6a0e0ef58ea300e263174c11@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The idea came to Kazuo Ishiguro when he was contemplating the 
 nuclear bomb. What would the 20th century have looked like\, he wondered\,
  "if the incredible developments that took place in nuclear physics\, culm
 inating in the creation of the atom and hydrogen bombs\, had taken place i
 nstead in the field of biology\, specifically in cloning." The result of h
 is questions is\nNever Let Me Go\n\, a beguiling masterpiece of science fi
 ction horror\, in which children at an elite boarding school in the 1990s 
 begin to realize that they aren't quite like other children. In this insta
 lment of "Classic Twists\," host Rebecca Panovka\, along with Joyce Carol 
 Oates and Jane Hu\, explores the mysteries of this most fragile and ambigu
 ous of Ishiguro's novels.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nJoyce Carol Oates\n
 is the author of over sixty novels\, including\nThem\n\,\nBlack Water\, Wh
 at I Lived For\, Blonde\n\, and most recently\nFox\n\, along with a number
  of plays and novellas\, and volumes of short stories\, poems and non-fict
 ion. The winner of the National Book Award\, two O. Henry Awards\, and the
  National Humanities Medal\, she is the Roger S. Berlind '52 Professor Eme
 rita in the Humanities with the Program in Creative Writing at Princeton U
 niversity.\nJane Hu\nis a literary critic whose work has appeared in\nThe 
 New Yorker\n\,\nThe New York Review of Books\n\,\nThe New York Times\n\, a
 nd\nHarper’s\n. She is an assistant professor of English at the Universi
 ty of Southern California.\nRebecca Panovka\nis a founder and editor of\nT
 he Drift\n\, a magazine of literature\, culture\, and politics. Her debut 
 novel\,\nArchival Research\n\, is forthcoming from Norton.\n***\nImportant
  information for accessing this live virtual event:\nYou must register for
  a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain access to this event.\nThis event wi
 ll be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive remi
 nder emails from both Zoom and Eventbrite with links and instructions to a
 ccess your unique Zoom Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of t
 he event\, you must be signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email
  address that you used to book your ticket in order to access your unique 
 event link. Unique event links can only be open on one device at a time.\n
 ***\nThis event is made possible in part from a gift from the Ann Thoburn 
 Fund.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public Library\nWe are committed to m
 aking our content accessible to as many Torontonians as possible. Please c
 all or email us if you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to req
 uest accommodation to participate in this program. Please let us know as f
 ar in advance as possible and we will do our best to meet your request. At
  least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email acces
 sibleservices@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a0e0ef58ea300e263174c11
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Art and Craft of Travel Writing with Author Will Ferguson
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T193000
UID:6a0e103c703c33630028dd03@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:From the Arctic Circle to the mud pools of New Zealand\,\nWill
  Ferguson\nhas spent a career chasing stories that reveal as much about Ca
 nada as they do about the world. The award-winning Canadian author will sh
 are insider tips from his twenty-five years in the trade and adventures fr
 om his latest book\,\nMeanwhile\, Back in Nokomis : Tales From Canada and 
 Elsewhere\n.\nAbout The Author\nWill Ferguson is an award-winning novelist
  and travel writer whose global adventures include trekking five hundred m
 iles across Northern Ireland\, road-tripping through Rwanda and hitchhikin
 g the length of Japan. He is a three-time winner of the Leacock Medal for 
 Humour\, and his novel 419 won the 2012 Giller Prize for Fiction. His work
  has been published in twenty-three languages. He lives in Calgary\, AB.\n
 Registration:\nThis virtual event is free to join and will take place on\n
 Zoom\n. Register to receive an email with details on how to join the sess
 ion.\nThis program will not be recorded.\nCaptions will be available.\nNee
 d help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZoom
  Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nM
 eanwhile\, Back in Nokomis : Tales From Canada and Elsewhere\n(book)\nOthe
 r books available at TPL\nby Will Ferguson.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a0e103c703c33630028dd03
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Information Session: The Private Sponsorship of Refugees Program
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260602T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260602T200000
UID:6a0f19bd8ea300e263177c57@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In collaboration with Toronto Public Library\, Refugee Sponsor
 ship Training Program (RSTP) offers an interactive session covering the Pr
 ivate Sponsorship of Refugees (PSR) Program\, including sponsorship eligib
 ility\, refugee admissibility\, and the One Year Window for family reunifi
 cation. Participants will leave with a clear understanding of eligibility 
 criteria\, the application process\, and required documentation.
LOCATION:Richview\, Room A\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a0f19bd8ea300e263177c57
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seniors Remember Yorkville: Documentaries and Discussion
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260623T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260623T200000
UID:6a0f4619f6bd012f00696526@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Get a glimpse of Yorkville from the early 20\nth\ncentury to t
 he 1950s through the memories and family stories of people who were there.
  Back Lane Studios presents Mapping Our Memories\, a project collecting pl
 ace-based stories from older people in video and written formats. You'll l
 earn about:\nGeorge Macey's grandfather\, who for decades managed an apart
 ment building on Bloor Street just east of Avenue Road. George's father wa
 s one of those kids who used to swim in the buff in the Don River. He late
 r ran a Yorkville gas station.\nVerna Van Sickle\, who opened a record sto
 re at the corner of Cumberland Avenue and Yonge Steet in the mid-1950s\, j
 ust as Yorkville was starting to attract artists and musicians. Verna had 
 many famous visitors drop by.\nSheila McIlraith\, whose father was a docto
 r and had his office in their home on Sherbourne Street. She used to vis
 it the Yorkville Library every week with her mother and brother.\nWe'll sc
 reen a curated selection of short documentaries\, learn about Mapping Our 
 Memories\, and meet some of the people behind the stories during a Q&A.\nN
 o registration required.
LOCATION:Yorkville\, Main floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a0f4619f6bd012f00696526
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
